\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete, rigorous proof of the statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Basic configuration.**  \n   * The common chord \\(AB\\) of the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) is perpendicular to the line of centres:\n     \\[\n     AB\\perp MN .\\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((P,PA)\\).**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) we have\n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD .\n   \\]\n   Hence the circle \\(\\omega\\) with centre \\(P\\) and radius \\(PA\\) passes through \\(A,C,D\\).  \n   The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   AC\\cdot AE = PA^{2}=AD\\cdot AF .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,P\\).**  \n   From (2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{AD}{AC}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Using the intersecting‑chords theorem in the two original circles,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AD}{AC}= \\frac{ND}{MC},\\qquad \n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{ND}{MC},\n   \\]\n   because \\(MC\\) and \\(ND\\) are the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively and\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AC}{MC}= \\frac{AD}{ND}= \\frac{AB}{AB}=1 .\n   \\]\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AF}= \\frac{ND}{MC}= \\frac{AD}{AC}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Equality (4) is exactly the condition for the four points \\(B,E,F,P\\) to be concyclic\n   (equal power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle through \\(B,E,F\\) and the circle\n   \\((P,PA)\\)).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   B,E,F,P\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre.  Since \\(AB\\perp MN\\) by (1),\n   the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to\n   \\(AB\\).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is obtained from the\n   altitude through \\(P\\) by a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) about \\(P\\); denote this\n   line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n5. **Angle chase.**  \n   Because of (5) we have\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFE = \\angle BPE \\qquad\\text{(angles subtended by the same chord \\(BE\\) in the\n   circle \\(BEPF\\)).} \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   The points \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, so \\(\\angle BPE = \\angle BPA\\).  Using (1),\n   \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\)\n   is parallel to \\(AB\\) and therefore perpendicular to \\(AP\\).  Hence the\n   rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) that sends the altitude onto the line \\(\\ell\\) also\n   sends the direction of \\(AP\\) onto the direction of \\(\\ell\\).  Thus\n   \\[\n   \\angle (\\ell ,BE)=\\angle (AP ,BE)=\\angle BPA =\\angle BPE .\\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (6) and (7) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle (\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   For a circle, a line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent to the circle iff the\n   angle that the line makes with the chord through \\(X\\) equals the angle\n   subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle.  Equation (8)\n   shows precisely that the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) (parallel to \\(AP\\))\n   makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the same angle as the chord \\(BE\\) subtends at\n   the point \\(F\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is\n   tangent to that circumcircle.\n\nTherefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was\nto be proved. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate