ndicular to the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) parallel to \\(AB\\); but the midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on \\(PA\\). Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\).\n\n6. **Relation between the required tangent line and the altitude.**  \n   * Let \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\). The altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) meets \\(MN\\) at a point we denote by \\(X\\); the line \\(PX\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\).  \n   * The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (call it \\(\\ell\\)) is obtained from the altitude \\(PX\\) by a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) about \\(P\\) (because in any triangle the segment joining the orthocenter to a vertex is the reflection of the opposite altitude in the corresponding side). Hence  \n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell,AP)=\\angle BPA .\\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Angle chase using the cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEPF\\).**  \n   * From the concyclicity proved in step 4, equal angles subtended by the same chord give  \n     \\[\n     \\angle BFE = \\angle BPE .\\tag{2}\n     \\]  \n   * Because \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BPE = \\angle BPA\\). Substituting into (2) yields  \n     \\[\n     \\angle BFE = \\angle BPA .\\tag{3}\n     \\]  \n   * Combining (1) and (3) we obtain  \n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell ,BE)=\\angle BPA =\\angle BFE .\\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n8. **Tangency.**  \n   * For a circle, a line through a point \\(X\\) is tangent to the circle iff the angle that the line makes with a chord through \\(X\\) equals the angle subtended by that chord at the opposite point of the circle.  \n   * Equation (4) shows precisely that the line \\(\\ell\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the same angle as the chord \\(BE\\) subtends at the point \\(F\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   * Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll configurations allowed by the hypothesis are covered; the case of t