e.  \n   * Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflection of the altitude \\(PX\\) in the side \\(PN\\) is the line \\(PH\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (call it \\(\\ell\\)) is obtained from \\(PX\\) by a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) about \\(P\\). Consequently\n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell ,AP)=\\angle BPA. \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Angle chase on the cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEPF\\).**  \n   * From step 4, \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic, so equal angles subtended by the same chord give\n     \\[\n     \\angle BFE = \\angle BPE. \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n   * Because \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BPE = \\angle BPA\\). Substituting into (2) yields\n     \\[\n     \\angle BFE = \\angle BPA. \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n8. **Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   * Let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (the first intersection is \\(B\\) only when \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(B\\); in general \\(T\\neq B\\)).  \n   * Using (1) and (3) we obtain\n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell ,BT)=\\angle(\\ell ,AP)=\\angle BPA=\\angle BFE. \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n   * Equality (4) is precisely the *tangent–chord theorem*: the angle between a line and a chord through the point of contact equals the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc. Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(T\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two intersecting circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)) with \\(r<R\\).  \nDenote by \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points, by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n-----------------------