\\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\); therefore it sends the circle \\(\\omega\\) onto a circle \\(\\omega'\\) that also passes through \\(D\\) and has the same centre‑angle \\(\\angle CPA\\) as \\(\\omega\\). The only circle with these two properties is \\(\\omega\\) itself; hence \\(\\omega'=\\omega\\) and the centre of \\(\\omega\\) is fixed. Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal S(P)=P}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) and the Miquel point\n\nThe four lines \\(AB,\\,CD,\\,AE,\\,AF\\) are pairwise distinct; no three of them are concurrent, so they form a complete quadrilateral.  \nThe circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) have the same centre \\(P\\) (by (5)), hence intersect exactly at \\(A\\) and at the Miquel point of the quadrilateral, which is \\(P\\).  \nBy the Miquel theorem the remaining two opposite vertices of the quadrilateral, namely \\(B\\) and the fourth diagonal point, lie on the same circle as \\(A,E,F,P\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,P\\text{ are concyclic}}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\)\n\nSince \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\) (because \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we know \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  \nConsider the isosceles trapezoid \\(ABCD\\): its bases are the parallel chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\), and its legs are \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\). In any isosceles trapezoid the segment joining the mid‑points of the bases is parallel to the legs. The midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on \\(PA\\) (by (7)), therefore the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) – i.e. the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) – is parallel to \\(AB\\). Denote this altitude by  \n\n\\[\nPX\\;(X\\in MN),\\qquad PX\\pa