responding side.  \n   * Applying this to \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the reflection of the altitude \\(PX\\) in the side \\(PN\\) is the line \\(PH\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (call it \\(\\ell\\)) is obtained from \\(PX\\) by a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) about \\(P\\). Consequently\n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell ,AP)=\\angle BPA. \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n7. **Angle chase on the cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEPF\\).**  \n   * From step 4, \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic, so equal angles subtended by the same chord give\n     \\[\n     \\angle BFE = \\angle BPE. \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n   * Because \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BPE = \\angle BPA\\). Substituting into (2) yields\n     \\[\n     \\angle BFE = \\angle BPA. \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n8. **Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   * Let \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (the first intersection is \\(B\\) only when \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(B\\); in general \\(T\\neq B\\)).  \n   * Using (1) and (3) we obtain\n     \\[\n     \\angle(\\ell ,BT)=\\angle(\\ell ,AP)=\\angle BPA=\\angle BFE. \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n   * Equality (4) is precisely the *tangent–chord theorem*: the angle between a line and a chord through the point of contact equals the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc. Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(T\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two intersecting circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)) with \\(r<R\\).  \nDenote by \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points, by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary facts\nBecause the circles intersect, the line of centres \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the common chord \\(AB\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad M,N\\text{ are symmetric w.r.t. }AB. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on opposite sides of the midpoint of \\(MN\\); consequently \\(C\\neq D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nThere exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nBy construction \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) maps each line through \\(A\\) onto itself, the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (call it \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (call it \\(F\\)). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal S(P)=? \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is defined as the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the circle  \n\n\\[\n\\omega\\;:=\\;(P,PA)\n\\]\n\npasses through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\).  \n\\(\\mathcal S\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\); therefore it sends the circle \\(\\omega\\) onto a circle \\(\\omega'\\) that also passes through \\(D\\) and has the same centre‑angle \\(\\angle CPA\\) as \\(\\omega\\). The only circle with these two properties is \\(\\omega\\) itself; hence \\(\\omega'=\\omega\\) and the centre of \\(\\omega\\) is fixed. Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal S(P)=P}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) and the Miquel point\n\nThe four lines \\(AB,\\,CD,\\,AE,\\,AF\\) are pairwise distinct; no three of them are concurrent, so they form a complete quadrilateral.  \nThe circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((AEF)\\) have the same centre \\(P\\) (by (5)), hence intersect exactly at \\(A\\) and at the Miquel point of the quadrilateral, which is \\(P\\).  \nBy the Miquel theorem the remaining two opposite vertices of the quadrilateral, namely \\(B\\) and the fourth diagonal point, lie on the same circle as \\(A,E,F,P\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B,E,F,P\\text{ are concyclic}}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\)\n\nSince \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of chord \\(CD\\) (because \\(P\\) is the centre of \\((ACD)\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA\\perp CD. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we know \\(AB\\perp MN\\).  \nConsider the isosceles trapezoid \\(ABCD\\): its bases are the parallel chords \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\), and its legs are \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\). In any isosceles trapezoid the segment joining the mid‑points of the bases is parallel to the legs. The midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on \\(PA\\) (by (7)), therefore the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) – i.e. the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) – is parallel to \\(AB\\). Denote this altitude by  \n\n\\[\nPX\\;(X\\in MN),\\qquad PX\\parallel AB. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Relation between the orthocenter \\(H\\) and the altitude\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nIn any triangle, the reflection of a vertex across the opposite side lies on the circumcircle; equivalently, the segment joining the orthocenter to a vertex is the reflection of the opposite altitude in the corresponding side.  \nReflecting the altitude \\(PX\\) in side \\(PN\\) yields the line \\(PH\\). Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (call it \\(\\ell\\)) is obtained from \\(PX\\) by a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) about \\(P\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle(\\ell ,AP)=\\angle BPA}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Angles on the circle \\((BEFP)\\)\n\nFrom the concyclicity (6) we have the equal angles subtended by chord \\(BF\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BPE. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BPE = \\angle BPA\\); inserting this into (10) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle BFE = \\angle BPA}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet \\(T\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) (the first intersection is \\(B\\) only when \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(B\\); in the generic configuration \\(T\\neq B\\)).  \n\nFrom (9) and (11) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,BT)=\\angle(\\ell ,AP)=\\angle BPA=\\angle BFE. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEquality (12) is precisely the *tangent–chord theorem*: the angle between a line and a chord through the point of contact equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite arc. Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎"
