) to the base \\(MN\\) of the isosceles triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).  \n    Hence **\\(PH\\perp MN\\)**.  \n    Together with (2) we obtain\n    \\[\n    PH\\parallel AP .\\tag{7}\n    \\]\n\n    The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is therefore exactly the line \\(PH\\).  \n    From (7) we get\n    \\[\n    \\angle\\bigl(\\,PH,\\,BP\\bigr)=\\angle APB .\\tag{8}\n    \\]\n\n    This is **Lemma 2**.\n\n6.  By Lemma 1,\n    \\[\n    \\angle BFE = \\angle BPA .\\tag{9}\n    \\]\n    By Lemma 2,\n    \\[\n    \\angle\\bigl(PH,\\,BP\\bigr)=\\angle APB .\\tag{10}\n    \\]\n    Since \\(\\angle BPA=\\angle APB\\), (9) and (10) give\n    \\[\n    \\angle\\bigl(PH,\\,BP\\bigr)=\\angle BFE .\n    \\]\n    The equality of the angle formed by the line \\(PH\\) with the chord \\(BF\\) and the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite arc is precisely the **tangent–chord theorem** for the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n    Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is \\(PH\\)) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R>r\\);  \n\nand let the two circles intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\nDenote by \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\).\nLet \\(P\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary facts\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, the line of centres \\(MN\\) is the perpendicular bisector of the common chord \\(AB\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN ,\\qquad A,B,M,N\\ \\text{are concyclic with centre the midpoint }O\\text{ of }MN .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,D\\in MN\\), the chord \\(CD\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\); the centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\((ACD)\\) therefore lies on the perpendicular to \\(MN\\) through \\(A\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nPA\\perp MN .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma 1 –  \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic  \n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and power \\(PA^{2}\\).\n\n* The circle \\((ACD)\\) is centred at \\(P\\); therefore it is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n* Any circle passing through the centre of inversion is transformed into a line not passing through the centre.  \n  Hence the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) become the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) which are the images of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.\n* Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the inversion circle (by (1) we have \\(PC=PA\\) and \\(PD=PA\\)), they are fixed points; consequently \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is the line through \\(C\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) the line through \\(D\\).\n* The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\) and satisfy \\(PE=PA\\) and \\(PF=PA\\) (they are the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the circles that pass through the centre of inversion); therefore they are also fixed by \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\nNow compute the power of the point \\(B\\) with respect to the circle \\((P)\\) of radius \\(PA\\).  \nFrom (2) we have \\(AB\\perp MN\\) and from (3) \\(PA\\perp MN\\); therefore the right‑angled triangles \\(PAB\\) and \\(PAC\\) share the altitude \\(AB\\) to the hypotenuse \\(PA\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nPB^{2}=PA^{2}-AB^{2}=PC^{2}-AC^{2}=PD^{2}-AD^{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line \\(AP\\), we also have  \n\n\\[\nPE^{2}=PA^{2}=PF^{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nCombining (4) and (5) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BPE=\\angle BPA,\\qquad \\angle BPF=\\angle BPA .\n\\]\n\nThus the four points \\(B,E,F,P\\) lie on a common circle; i.e.  \n\n\\[\nB,E,F,P\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) we obtain the useful angle relation  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BPA .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 2 –  direction of the line through the orthocentre  \n\nFrom (3) we know that \\(PA\\perp MN\\); hence the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(MN\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(PM=PN\\) (both equal the radius \\(PA\\) of the circle \\((P)\\) that contains the fixed points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the line \\(MN\\)), the point \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nIn any triangle the vector from the circum‑centre to the orthocentre equals the sum of the vectors from the circum‑centre to the three vertices:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}+\\overrightarrow{PO}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\nBecause \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{PO}= \\tfrac12(\\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN})\\); substituting in (8) gives\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{PH}= \\tfrac32(\\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe vector \\(\\overrightarrow{PM}+\\overrightarrow{PN}\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\) (it is twice the median from \\(P\\) to the base \\(MN\\) of the isosceles triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AP .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(PH\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(PH,\\,BP\\bigr)=\\angle APB .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have (7): \\(\\displaystyle \\angle BFE = \\angle BPA\\).  \nFrom Lemma 2 we have (12): \\(\\displaystyle \\angle\\bigl(PH,\\,BP\\bigr)=\\angle APB\\).\n\nSince \\(\\angle BPA = \\angle APB\\) (they are the same oriented angle), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(PH,\\,BP\\bigr)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nThe equality of the angle formed by the line \\(PH\\) with the chord \\(BF\\) and the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite arc is exactly the **tangent–chord theorem** for the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which is \\(PH\\)) is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\nThus the required statement is proved. ∎"
